33.668 341.462]/StructParent 225/Subj(Highlight)/Subtype/Highlight/T(Kate Murray)/Type/Annot>>endobj1756 0 obj<</F 28/Open false/Parent 1755 0 R/Rect[493.228 249.019 677.228 341.019]/Subtype/Popup/Type/Annot>>endobj1757 0 obj<</AP<</N 1759 0 R/R 1760 0 R>>/C[0.588242 0.262741 0.988235]/Contents(So some  key benefits are:\r\r1. geothermal power plants are far less emissions intensive than fossil fuel-based power plants, but additionally you can add in BECCS \(which you've shown to be more efficient than DAC\) which results in net negative emissio\ns. \rQ: I take it that other renewable energy sources don't have carbon sequestration options affiliated with them? \r\r2. CDR is essential for meeting net zero targets, particularly in countries like NZ that are high per capita emitters but for whom key\ industrial contributors are not fully addresed by renewable electricity alone \(i.e.., agriculture\). \rQ: are there other countries for whom this is true? You highlight that NZ has a high proportion of renewable energy. Is this uniquely beneficial in NZ\ or do you think other countries would benefit in meeting carbon net zero targets from using a similar approach?\r\r3. Other leading CDR strategies are particularly vulnerable to the effects of climate change \(fire and drought affecting afforestation\), \so this is an important alternate strategy\rQ: Are there similar durability risks for this approach?\rQ: how many countries would be positioned to use this approach? It does seem like it's a minority of countries using geothermal strategies. Thoughts on s\calability for global roll out?)/CreationDate(D:20241015121526+13'00')/F 28/M(D:20241015122746+13'00')/NM(20412b0f-c383-48c1-833b-2dddb78210c1)/Name/Comment/P 14 0 R/Popup 1758 0 R/RC(<?xml version="1.0"?><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:24.3.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2" ><p dir="ltr"><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:10.5pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;f\ont-style:normal">So some  key benefits are:&#13;&#13;1. geothermal power plants are far less emissions intensive than fossil fuel-based power plants, but additionally you can add in BECCS \(which you've shown to be more efficient than DAC\) which results\ in net negative emissions. &#13;Q: I take it that other renewable energy sources don't have carbon sequestration options affiliated with them? &#13;&#13;2. CDR is essential for meeting net zero targets, particularly in countries like NZ that are high per\ capita emitters but for whom key industrial contributors are not fully addresed by renewable electricity alone \(i.e.., agriculture\). &#13;Q: are there other countries for whom this is true? You highlight that NZ has a high proportion of renewable energ\y. Is this uniquely beneficial in NZ or do you think other countries would benefit in meeting carbon net zero targets from using a similar approach?&#13;&#13;3. Other leading CDR strategies are particularly vulnerable to the effects of climate change \(fi\re and drought affecting afforestation\), so this is an important alternate strategy&#13;Q: Are there similar durability risks for this approach?&#13;Q: how many countries would be positioned to use this approach? It does seem like it's a minority of coun\tries using geothermal strategies. Thoughts on scalability for global roll out?</span></p></body>)/Rect[427.202 399.718 451.202 423.718]/Subj(Sticky Note)/Subtype/Text/T(Kate Murray)/Type/Annot>>endobj1758 0 obj<</F 28/Open true/Parent 1757 0 R/Rect[433.966 233.681 617.894 423.5]/Subtype/Popup/Type/Annot>>endobj1759 0 obj<</BBox[0.0 0.0 24.0 24.0]/Filter/FlateDecode/Length 257/Resources<</ExtGState<</GS0<</AIS false/BM/Normal/CA 0.6/Type/ExtGState/ca 0.6>>>>>>/Subtype/Form/Type/XObject>>stream
